WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.115 --> 00:00:06.368
- Day to day living I find
can be pretty mundane,

00:00:06.368 --> 00:00:07.776
and you know when I'm out there,

00:00:07.776 --> 00:00:09.599
you've just got a really simple task,

00:00:09.599 --> 00:00:12.516
which is to survive and enjoy life.

00:00:17.379 --> 00:00:22.035
My name is Gavin McClurg, and
I'm a cross-country paraglider.

00:00:22.035 --> 00:00:22.868
Whew!

00:00:23.891 --> 00:00:26.877
We're basically flying a
modified bedsheet and strings.

00:00:26.877 --> 00:00:30.824
We don't have a motor and
we're just using thermals.

00:00:30.824 --> 00:00:33.715
Thermals are kind of
like the gas for a car.

00:00:33.715 --> 00:00:35.176
We get as high as we possibly can

00:00:35.176 --> 00:00:37.480
and then we glide and if
we find another thermal,

00:00:37.480 --> 00:00:38.980
we can keep going.

00:00:42.355 --> 00:00:44.627
I've flown in the Himalayas;
I've flown in Morocco;

00:00:44.627 --> 00:00:46.781
I've flown all over the Alps in Europe,

00:00:46.781 --> 00:00:49.363
in the Canadian Rockies
and we're just looking

00:00:49.363 --> 00:00:53.530
at a map in a completely
different way these days.

00:01:00.317 --> 00:01:02.909
Hurricane Ridge is one of
the most spectacular places

00:01:02.909 --> 00:01:04.736
for me in the world.

00:01:04.736 --> 00:01:06.048
There's this beautiful long ridge

00:01:06.048 --> 00:01:08.405
that ties right in with the Grand Canyon.

00:01:08.405 --> 00:01:12.405
It sits right on the edge
of Zion National Park.

00:01:15.264 --> 00:01:16.757
I was doing this expedition with some

00:01:16.757 --> 00:01:18.251
of the best pilots in the world,

00:01:18.251 --> 00:01:21.845
and right as the sun set, you know,

00:01:21.845 --> 00:01:23.509
the conditions just went bang on

00:01:23.509 --> 00:01:26.069
and we all launched into the sky

00:01:26.069 --> 00:01:28.736
and, you know, paragliding
can be really intense

00:01:28.736 --> 00:01:31.339
and sometimes really
scary and very dangerous,

00:01:31.339 --> 00:01:32.779
and this was like the opposite of that.

00:01:32.779 --> 00:01:36.811
It was just playful and
fun and we were doing

00:01:36.811 --> 00:01:40.850
huge wingovers and acro
and when I'm flying

00:01:40.850 --> 00:01:43.357
over these really remote places,

00:01:43.357 --> 00:01:45.650
I think what it gives you is a perspective

00:01:45.650 --> 00:01:49.817
and an awe that is impossible
to get in any other way.

00:01:54.993 --> 00:01:57.169
These guys and I are
people that, you know,

00:01:57.169 --> 00:01:59.867
we really chase records
and we chase huge distances

00:01:59.867 --> 00:02:01.851
and made us realize that, you know,

00:02:01.851 --> 00:02:04.667
it's not about the records
and it's not about flying

00:02:04.667 --> 00:02:07.110
huge distances, it's just about enjoying

00:02:07.110 --> 00:02:08.943
the freedom of flight.

